// #4408. Pins GC RETENTION — the `swept=`/`live=` SPLIT of a `BIT_GC_STATS=1`
// exit summary — so a change in what the collector keeps is attributed to the
// commit that causes it instead of being found later by a bisect.
//
// #4402 watched `live` move 22% and `swept` 79% on bench/cases/json between two
// releases while every differential, golden, stress and benchgate arm stayed
// green. 941,761 objects (46.25 MB) were false roots: `copyBytes`
// (runtime/root/root.bit) spilled ten pointer-shaped callee-saved words into a
// 112-byte frame, and runtime/stw/stwscan.bit's `stwScanStackRange` scans
// `[sp, top)` conservatively on every collection, so those words were read as
// live references. Making the leaf a leaf stopped writing them. THAT direction
// was safe; the same blind spot covers the dangerous ones — over-retention is
// an RSS bill nobody attributes, and under-retention is a use-after-free.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT test-benchgate. That gate pins `swept + live` (#3130's
// `Baseline.objects`) — every object the process ever allocated. Across #4402's
// two builds `swept + live` was IDENTICAL (5,400,062) while the SPLIT moved by
// 941,761 objects. A total-allocation pin is structurally blind to retention.
// This gate reads the split and nothing else; the two are complementary, not
// overlapping.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ASSERTION SHAPE, AND THE MEASUREMENTS THAT CHOSE IT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #4408 required the shape to be measured rather than picked. Readings taken on
// `beab7b12`, aarch64-macos, on a DELIBERATELY LOADED box (load average 67 on
// 18 cores, five other agents building) — because a counter that is
// load-sensitive cannot be pinned at all, and the loaded box is the adversarial
// case, not a caveat:
//
//   1. `swept` and `live` are BYTE-EXACT across repetitions. 7/7 identical on
//      bench/cases/{alloc,json,sort}, 5/5 on each of this gate's own two
//      fixtures, 5/5 under BIT_GC=stress. Zero spread, every arm.
//
//   2. `collections` is NOT stable and must never be pinned: sort at
//      BIT_GC_MIN_KB=512 read 4,4,5,5,5 over five runs, and the stress arm read
//      20122/20123 and 40226/40227/40230. The PACING is nondeterministic under
//      load; the RETENTION is not. That contrast is the evidence for (1) rather
//      than a coincidence of a quiet box.
//
//   3. `peakbytes` is NOT byte-stable: json at BIT_GC_MIN_KB=512 read
//      185135104 four times and 185266176 once (+0.07%, one 128 KB chunk). An
//      RSS ratchet would therefore need a band whose width is a guess, and it
//      is ~1000x coarser than a counter that has no spread at all. Rejected.
//
//   4. `swept`/`live` DO move with BIT_GC_MIN_KB — alloc read live=25017 at
//      4096, 10008 at 512 and 305185 at 65536 — so every row here sets
//      BIT_GC_MIN_KB EXPLICITLY. A row that inherited the default would move
//      silently the day the default moves. (#4402 saw json invariant from 2 to
//      31 collections; that is json's workload, not a general property.)
//
// So: EXACT TWO-SIDED PINS, not a band and not an RSS ratchet. There is no
// measured jitter for a band to accommodate, and a band wide enough to be safe
// is wide enough to miss the 22% move this gate exists to catch. An exact pin
// cannot go stale unnoticed the way a ratchet can (#3713, #4099): it reds on the
// commit that moves it, which IS the attribution this ticket asked for. When it
// reds for a legitimate reason, re-derive the row with the one-line command in
// its comment and say in the commit message what moved and why.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TWO ARMS, AND WHY BOTH
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PACED (`pacedRows`): BIT_GC_MIN_KB pinned, `swept` and `live` pinned exactly.
// This is the production regime — the split that decides the RSS bill. Its
// numbers are PER-PLATFORM (benchgate's convention, see baselinesdefs.bit): only
// aarch64-macos is measured, and an unmeasured host skips the pins loudly, with
// every measured value printed so recording the row takes one run.
//
// STRESS (`stressRows`): BIT_GC=stress collects at every safepoint, so `live` at
// exit is the EXACT reachable set with no floating garbage. Asserting `live == 0`
// needs no baseline and holds on every platform by construction: a fixture that
// drops all of its garbage retains none of it. This arm is what makes the gate
// non-vacuous on a host with no paced table, and it is the arm a false root
// cannot survive: #4408's mutation proof widened `stwScanOwnStack`'s
// conservative range 4096 bytes BELOW sp, into dead frames — the exact shape
// #4402 diagnosed — and this arm reported the retention it created.
//
// `live == 0` alone WOULD be vacuous — a program allocating nothing passes it —
// so every stress row also demands `swept >= sweptFloor`, a conservative floor
// under the fixture's own measured total. Both must hold.
//
// If a future runtime change makes some boot object permanently reachable,
// `live` becomes that constant and this arm reds. That is a legitimate red and
// the right conversation: an object live at process exit is retention. Update
// the row, do not widen it into a band.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COST
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Five builds and seven runs. Measured 7.5s of user CPU / 12.0s of harness-
// reported wall clock on `beab7b12`, on a box carrying five other agents
// (`./make test` is ~23 minutes). The stress arm is the expensive half and its cost is
// O(objects x live set), NOT linear: bench/cases/json under stress is 42.6s at
// 3000 records and would be hours at its real 150000, which is why the stress
// arm uses this gate's own small fixtures and the bench cases appear only in the
// paced arm. Do not raise a stress fixture's N without re-timing it.
//
// Run it from the repository root:
//     ./bit-out/bin/bit run _tests_/bit/gcretention.bit
// `BIT_SELF_EXE` overrides which `bit` is exercised; `BIT_STDLIB` must point at
// a real stdlib (the fixtures import std/strings); `BIT_BENCH_DIR` must point at
// bench/cases (required, never defaulted — a harness that silently walked the
// wrong directory would report a confident verdict about nothing).
import { env, envOr, exit } from "std/os"
import { exists, isDir, mkdir, readFile, writeFile } from "std/fs"
import { parseInt } from "std/strings"
import { monotonic, since, Millisecond } from "std/time"
import { uuidV4, format as uuidFormat } from "std/uuid"

class Tally { checks: int, failures: int }

fn check(t: Tally, cond: bool, label: string) {
  t.checks = t.checks + 1
  if (!cond) {
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
    eprint("FAIL: ${label}\n")
  }
}

fn timeoutMs(): int {
  let s = parseInt(envOr("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S", "300")) catch _ {
    300
  }
  if (s <= 0) {
    return 300000
  }
  return s * 1000
}

fn shell(scriptPath: string, script: string, ms: int): int! {
  writeFile(scriptPath, script)?
  if (fsChmod(scriptPath, 448) != 0) { // 0o700
    fail newError("gcretention: cannot make script executable: ${scriptPath}")
  }
  return osRunBounded(scriptPath, ms)
}

// The decimal that follows the FIRST occurrence of `key`, or -1 when `key` is
// absent or not followed by a digit. -1 rather than 0 is load-bearing: a
// missing field must fail this gate, never read as "zero of it happened".
fn intAfter(s: string, key: string): int {
  let n = len(key)
  let i = 0
  while (i + n <= len(s)) {
    if (s[i:i + n] == key) {
      let j = i + n
      let v = 0
      let digits = 0
      while (j < len(s) && s[j] >= u8('0') && s[j] <= u8('9')) {
        v = v * 10 + (int(s[j]) - int(u8('0')))
        digits = digits + 1
        j = j + 1
      }
      if (digits == 0) {
        return 0 - 1
      }
      return v
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0 - 1
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fixtures this gate owns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// #4402's own smallest reproduction of the false-root retention, verbatim
// apart from N: a std/strings builder fed an INTERPOLATED string. Both halves
// are required — interpolation alone shows no difference, and a builder fed a
// constant string shows no difference; together they drive `copyBytes`, the
// runtime leaf whose frame held the raw heap addresses.
const builderN: int = 20000

fn builderSource(): string {
  return "import { newBuilder } from \"std/strings\"\n\n" +
    "fn main() {\n" +
    "  let b = newBuilder()\n" +
    "  let i = 0\n" +
    "  while (i < ${builderN}) {\n" +
    "    b.write(\"user\${i}-\${i}\")\n" +
    "    i = i + 1\n" +
    "  }\n" +
    "  print(\"\${len(b.toString())}\\n\")\n" +
    "}\n"
}

// bench/cases/alloc's shape, reduced so the stress arm stays under a second:
// batches of short-lived nodes, each an independent heap object, summed and
// dropped. `owner` is a REFERENCE field and is not decoration — a class whose
// fields are all scalars is packed inline into the slice's own buffer (#3862,
// runtime/ABI.md §2) and allocates no per-element objects at all, which would
// quietly turn this fixture into one that measures nothing.
const churnBatches: int = 40
const churnPer: int = 500

fn churnSource(): string {
  return "class Batch { id: i64 }\n" +
    "class Node { x: i64, y: i64, owner: Batch }\n\n" +
    "fn main() {\n" +
    "  let total = 0\n" +
    "  let b = 0\n" +
    "  while (b < ${churnBatches}) {\n" +
    "    let owner = Batch{ id: b }\n" +
    "    let nodes = []Node(0, ${churnPer})\n" +
    "    let k = 0\n" +
    "    while (k < ${churnPer}) {\n" +
    "      nodes = append(nodes, Node{ x: b + k, y: k + 1, owner: owner })\n" +
    "      k = k + 1\n" +
    "    }\n" +
    "    for nd of nodes {\n" +
    "      total = total + nd.x + nd.y + nd.owner.id\n" +
    "    }\n" +
    "    b = b + 1\n" +
    "  }\n" +
    "  print(\"\${total}\\n\")\n" +
    "}\n"
}

// One measured row. `source` empty means bench/cases/<name>/<name>.bit; a
// non-empty `source` is written to scratch and built from there.
//
// PACED row (`stress: false`): `minKb` is exported as BIT_GC_MIN_KB and `swept`
// / `live` are exact two-sided pins. -1 in either means "not measured on this
// platform" and skips the pin, exactly as benchgate's `objects: -1` does.
//
// STRESS row (`stress: true`): BIT_GC=stress, `live` must be 0 and `swept` must
// be at least `sweptFloor`. `minKb`, `swept` and `live` are unused.
class Row {
  name: string,
  source: string,
  stress: bool,
  minKb: int,
  swept: int,
  live: int,
  sweptFloor: int,
}

// The stress arm. Platform-independent by construction — no baseline, so these
// rows run and can FAIL on every host, including one with no paced table.
//
// The floors are ~83% and ~50% of the totals measured on `beab7b12`
// (builder 60025, churn 20126); they exist only to stop `live == 0` passing
// vacuously on a fixture that stopped allocating, so a loose floor is correct
// here and a tight one would be a second unpinned baseline.
fn stressRows(): []Row {
  return [
    Row{name: "builder", source: builderSource(), stress: true, minKb: 0,
      swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1, sweptFloor: 50000},
    Row{name: "churn", source: churnSource(), stress: true, minKb: 0,
      swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1, sweptFloor: 10000},
  ]
}

// The paced arm, macOS/aarch64. Every number below was read on `beab7b12` and
// was identical on every repetition (7 for each bench case, 5 for each owned
// fixture). RE-DERIVE ANY ROW WITH ONE COMMAND, from the repository root:
//
//   BIT_GC_MIN_KB=4096 BIT_GC_STATS=1 ./bit-out/bin/bit run bench/cases/alloc/alloc.bit
//
// (substitute the row's own minKb and path; the owned fixtures print their
// generated source path in this gate's own output). Read `swept=` and `live=`
// off the `[bit-gc]` line on stderr. Nothing else is needed and no script
// exists for it — that is the point of an exactly deterministic counter.
//
// The bench cases carry 4096 because that is the documented default
// (runtime/ABI.md), verified here to give byte-identical counters to an unset
// BIT_GC_MIN_KB; the owned fixtures carry 512 because at 4096 churn never
// collects at all (collections=0, swept=0) and a row that never sweeps cannot
// see a retention change.
fn pacedRowsMacosArm64(): []Row {
  return [
    Row{name: "builder", source: builderSource(), stress: false, minKb: 512,
      swept: 56890, live: 3135, sweptFloor: 0},
    Row{name: "churn", source: churnSource(), stress: false, minKb: 512,
      swept: 10567, live: 9559, sweptFloor: 0},
    Row{name: "alloc", source: "", stress: false, minKb: 4096,
      swept: 9980989, live: 25017, sweptFloor: 0},
    Row{name: "json", source: "", stress: false, minKb: 4096,
      swept: 1200034, live: 4200027, sweptFloor: 0},
    Row{name: "sort", source: "", stress: false, minKb: 4096,
      swept: 349619, live: 250403, sweptFloor: 0},
  ]
}

// Keyed on `hostTarget()`'s ordinal — BOTH platform and architecture, never
// `uname -s` alone, for the reason benchgate's `baselinesFor` gives: an
// aarch64-linux run compared against an x86_64-linux table produces a
// confidently wrong verdict.
fn pacedRowsFor(target: int): []Row {
  if (target == 2) {
    return pacedRowsMacosArm64()
  }
  return []
}

fn platformName(target: int): string {
  if (target == 0) {
    return "linux/x86_64"
  }
  if (target == 1) {
    return "linux/aarch64"
  }
  if (target == 2) {
    return "macos/aarch64"
  }
  return "unknown hostTarget() ordinal ${target}"
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measurement
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Stats { ok: bool, exitCode: int, swept: int, live: int }

// Everything a build/run step needs that does NOT vary per row — grouped so no
// function below carries more than 5 parameters (E0202).
class Ctx { t: Tally, bit: string, stdlib: string, benchDir: string, scratch: string, ms: int }

fn bitBinary(): string! {
  let explicit = env("BIT_SELF_EXE")
  if (len(explicit) > 0) {
    if (!exists(explicit)) {
      fail newError("gcretention: BIT_SELF_EXE=${explicit} does not exist")
    }
    return explicit
  }
  let candidate = "./bit-out/bin/bit"
  if (!exists(candidate)) {
    fail newError("gcretention: no compiler at ${candidate}; run this from the repository root or set BIT_SELF_EXE")
  }
  return candidate
}

// The `.bit` this row builds. An owned fixture is written to scratch under a
// name that includes the arm, so the paced and stress copies of one fixture
// never race for a path.
fn sourcePath(ctx: Ctx, row: Row, tag: string): string! {
  if (len(row.source) == 0) {
    let p = "${ctx.benchDir}/${row.name}/${row.name}.bit"
    if (!exists(p)) {
      fail newError("gcretention: no bench case at ${p} (BIT_BENCH_DIR=${ctx.benchDir})")
    }
    return p
  }
  let p = "${ctx.scratch}/${tag}.bit"
  writeFile(p, row.source)?
  return p
}

fn buildFixture(ctx: Ctx, src: string, prog: string, tag: string): int! {
  let script = "#!/bin/sh\n" +
    "BIT_STDLIB=${ctx.stdlib}\nexport BIT_STDLIB\n" +
    "'${ctx.bit}' build '${src}' -o '${prog}'\n"
  return shell("${ctx.scratch}/build_${tag}.sh", script, ctx.ms)?
}

// The GC configuration this row is measured under, as `sh` export lines. The
// two arms are mutually exclusive: a stress row sets BIT_GC and no minimum, a
// paced row sets the minimum and never BIT_GC.
fn gcExports(row: Row): string {
  if (row.stress) {
    return "BIT_GC=stress\nexport BIT_GC\n"
  }
  return "BIT_GC_MIN_KB=${row.minKb}\nexport BIT_GC_MIN_KB\n"
}

fn runFixture(ctx: Ctx, row: Row, prog: string, tag: string): Stats! {
  let errPath = "${ctx.scratch}/err_${tag}.txt"
  let script = "#!/bin/sh\n" + gcExports(row) +
    "BIT_GC_STATS=1\nexport BIT_GC_STATS\n" +
    "exec '${prog}' > '${ctx.scratch}/out_${tag}.txt' 2> '${errPath}'\n"
  let code = shell("${ctx.scratch}/run_${tag}.sh", script, ctx.ms)?
  let err = readFile(errPath) catch _ {
    ""
  }
  let swept = intAfter(err, "swept=")
  let live = intAfter(err, "live=")
  if (swept < 0 || live < 0) {
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: code, swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1 }
  }
  return Stats{ ok: true, exitCode: code, swept: swept, live: live }
}

// Builds and runs one row, returning its retention counters. `ok` false means
// the failing checks are already recorded on `ctx.t` and the caller must assert
// nothing further — a row that could not be measured is a FAIL, never a skip.
fn measure(ctx: Ctx, row: Row, tag: string): Stats {
  let src = sourcePath(ctx, row, tag) catch e {
    check(ctx.t, false, "${tag}: ${e.message()}")
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: 0 - 1, swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1 }
  }
  let prog = "${ctx.scratch}/${tag}"
  let build = buildFixture(ctx, src, prog, tag) catch e {
    check(ctx.t, false, "${tag}: ${e.message()}")
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: 0 - 1, swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1 }
  }
  check(ctx.t, build == 0 && exists(prog), "${tag}: ${src} must compile (bit build exited ${build})")
  if (build != 0 || !exists(prog)) {
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: 0 - 1, swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1 }
  }
  let st = runFixture(ctx, row, prog, tag) catch e {
    check(ctx.t, false, "${tag}: ${e.message()}")
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: 0 - 1, swept: 0 - 1, live: 0 - 1 }
  }
  check(ctx.t, st.exitCode == 0, "${tag}: must exit 0 (exited ${st.exitCode})")
  check(ctx.t, st.ok, "${tag}: no `[bit-gc] ... swept=N live=N` summary — the run was not measured")
  if (st.exitCode != 0 || !st.ok) {
    return Stats{ ok: false, exitCode: st.exitCode, swept: st.swept, live: st.live }
  }
  return st
}

// BIT_GC=stress arm. `live == 0` is the whole assertion; `swept >= sweptFloor`
// is what stops it passing vacuously.
fn evalStressRow(ctx: Ctx, row: Row) {
  let tag = "${row.name}_stress"
  let st = measure(ctx, row, tag)
  if (!st.ok) {
    return
  }
  print("gcretention: ${tag}: swept=${st.swept} live=${st.live} (want live=0, swept>=${row.sweptFloor})\n")
  check(
    ctx.t, st.live == 0,
    "${tag}: live=${st.live}, want 0 — under BIT_GC=stress every safepoint collects, so a non-zero live set at exit is retained garbage (swept=${st.swept})",
  )
  check(
    ctx.t, st.swept >= row.sweptFloor,
    "${tag}: swept=${st.swept}, want at least ${row.sweptFloor} — the collector reclaimed too little for `live == 0` to mean anything",
  )
}

// Paced arm. Exact two-sided pins; a -1 pin is an unmeasured platform and the
// measured value is printed so recording it takes one run.
fn evalPacedRow(ctx: Ctx, row: Row) {
  let tag = "${row.name}_min${row.minKb}"
  let st = measure(ctx, row, tag)
  if (!st.ok) {
    return
  }
  print("gcretention: ${tag}: swept=${st.swept} live=${st.live} (want swept=${row.swept} live=${row.live})\n")
  if (row.swept < 0 || row.live < 0) {
    return
  }
  check(
    ctx.t, st.swept == row.swept,
    "${tag}: swept=${st.swept}, pinned ${row.swept} — retention moved; re-derive with BIT_GC_MIN_KB=${row.minKb} BIT_GC_STATS=1 and say in the commit message what moved",
  )
  check(
    ctx.t, st.live == row.live,
    "${tag}: live=${st.live}, pinned ${row.live} — retention moved; re-derive with BIT_GC_MIN_KB=${row.minKb} BIT_GC_STATS=1 and say in the commit message what moved",
  )
}

fn benchDir(): string! {
  let d = env("BIT_BENCH_DIR")
  if (len(d) == 0) {
    fail newError("gcretention: BIT_BENCH_DIR is unset — it is required, never defaulted")
  }
  if (!isDir(d)) {
    fail newError("gcretention: BIT_BENCH_DIR=${d} is not a directory")
  }
  return d
}

fn newCtx(t: Tally, scratch: string, ms: int): Ctx! {
  let stdlib = env("BIT_STDLIB")
  if (len(stdlib) == 0) {
    fail newError("gcretention: BIT_STDLIB is unset — this gate's fixtures import stdlib")
  }
  return Ctx{ t: t, bit: bitBinary()?, stdlib: stdlib, benchDir: benchDir()?,
    scratch: scratch, ms: ms }
}

fn runAll(t: Tally, scratch: string, ms: int) {
  let ctx = newCtx(t, scratch, ms) catch e {
    check(t, false, e.message())
    return
  }
  for row of stressRows() {
    evalStressRow(ctx, row)
  }
  let target = hostTarget()
  let paced = pacedRowsFor(target)
  if (len(paced) == 0) {
    print("gcretention: NOTICE — no paced baseline recorded for ${platformName(target)}; " +
      "the stress arm still ran. Record the rows from a run's printed values, in pacedRowsFor().\n")
    return
  }
  for row of paced {
    evalPacedRow(ctx, row)
  }
}

fn main() {
  let started = monotonic()
  let ms = timeoutMs()
  let tmp = trimSuffix(envOr("TMPDIR", "/tmp"), "/")
  let scratch = "${tmp}/bit-gcretention-${uuidFormat(uuidV4())}"
  let t = Tally{ checks: 0, failures: 0 }

  mkdir(scratch) catch e {
    panic("gcretention: cannot create ${scratch}: ${e.message()}")
  }
  runAll(t, scratch, ms)

  // The stress arm alone contributes 8 checks on every platform (2 rows x
  // build/exit/summary/live/swept, less the summary check folded into `ok`), so
  // a run that recorded fewer than 8 did not measure what it claims to.
  if (t.checks < 8) {
    eprint("FAIL: only ${t.checks} checks ran, want at least 8\n")
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
  }
  let elapsedMs = since(started) / Millisecond
  if (t.failures > 0) {
    // `exit()` does not run pending defers (#3694) — tear down explicitly on
    // both the PASS and the FAIL path.
    teardown(scratch, ms)
    println("gcretention: FAIL — ${t.failures} of ${t.checks} checks failed in ${elapsedMs}ms")
    exit(1)
  }
  teardown(scratch, ms)
  println("gcretention: PASS — ${t.checks} checks in ${elapsedMs}ms")
}

fn teardown(scratch: string, ms: int) {
  shell("${scratch}/rm.sh", "#!/bin/sh\nrm -rf '${scratch}'\n", ms) catch _ {
    return
  }
}

fn trimSuffix(s: string, suffix: string): string {
  if (len(suffix) > 0 && len(s) >= len(suffix) && s[len(s) - len(suffix):] == suffix) {
    return s[:len(s) - len(suffix)]
  }
  return s
}
